\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The normalized weight is a probability measure}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{For a \vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004} #{Q}
on a finite real inner-product space #{(E,h)}, the formula for its
\vocabk{Gaussian probability law}{fdip-0005} defines a Borel
probability measure. If #{\dim E=0}, then #{S_Q=0}, #{Z_Q=1} and
#{\mu_Q=\delta_0}. If the same normalization construction uses
#{c\lambda_E} in place of #{\lambda_E}, with #{0<c<\infty}, its
normalizer and law satisfy
##{\widetilde Z_Q=cZ_Q,\qquad\widetilde\mu_Q=\mu_Q.}}
\proof{
\p{The weight is positive Borel by \ref{fdip-00JQ}.
Nonnegativity and the empty-set axiom
follow from the nonnegative integral. For pairwise disjoint Borel
#{A_k}, the functions #{w_Q\sum_{k=1}^N\mathbf1_{A_k}} increase to
#{w_Q\mathbf1_{\bigcup_k A_k}}. Monotone convergence and finite
additivity of the integral give countable additivity after division
by #{Z_Q}. The total mass is #{Z_Q/Z_Q=1}, using the positive finite
normalizer in \ref{fdip-002T}.}
\p{In dimension zero, #{E} is the singleton #{\{0\}} with canonical
mass one. Its action is zero and its weight is one, so #{Z_Q=1}
and the displayed set formula is #{\delta_0}. For #{0<c<\infty},
linearity in the reference measure gives
##{\int_A w_Q\,d(c\lambda_E)=c\int_A w_Q\,d\lambda_E.}
Taking #{A=E} gives #{\widetilde Z_Q=cZ_Q}. Dividing the set formula
by #{cZ_Q} cancels #{c} and proves equality of the normalized laws
on every Borel set.}
}
\p{Countable additivity uses
\citet{Theorem 1.4.44, pp. 107-108, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
The reference-rescaling example \ref{fdip-002N} displays the same
cancellation. Mathlib's \leanref{MeasureTheory.Measure.withDensity}
constructs the measure with #{Z_Q^{-1}w_Q} viewed as an extended
nonnegative density.}
